## Product delivery

The bar for anything a user sees or touches. Not agent chat style.

### Every interactive surface

Cover all four states before calling a screen done: loading, empty, error, success. A missing state is unfinished work.

- Every async action shows progress and cannot be double-submitted.
- Every failure tells the user what happened and what to do next. Never a raw stack trace, a bare error code, or a silent no-op.
- Destructive or irreversible actions require explicit confirmation that names the target.
- Form errors appear next to the field, in plain language, and say how to fix it.
- Never lose user input on error, refresh, or back navigation.

### Accessible by default

- Every control is reachable and operable by keyboard and has a visible focus state.
- Every input has a label; every icon-only button has an accessible name; every meaningful image has alt text.
- Never use color alone to carry meaning.
- Body text meets WCAG AA contrast (4.5:1).

### Clarity

- Labels and buttons name the outcome ("Save changes"), not the mechanism ("Submit").
- Copy is specific: say what happened, to what, and what comes next.
- The primary action on a screen is visually obvious and unambiguous.
- Format dates, times, numbers, and currency for the user's locale and timezone. Never render a raw timestamp or unformatted number.

### Defaults over knobs

- Ship one good default. Add configuration only when two real, present use cases need different values.
- Do not hardcode values a deployment genuinely needs to change (limits, thresholds, windows) — put them in config.

### Delivery bar

- Ship a complete path: a user can start the task and finish it.
- Spend depth on the core flow; keep everything else plain.
- Never present a stub, mock data, or a disabled control as a finished feature.
- If the full path cannot ship, say what is missing before you build it, not after.

### Do not

- Add a dashboard, settings page, or tab to a single-purpose surface.
- Add process, screens, or abstraction the user did not ask for.
- Show internal identifiers, enum names, or debug output to users.
